::ENG3_U06_L06_GP01::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student is preparing a research paragraph for a class LAN page.
What should the student do first?{
~A. Choose the largest image available#Incorrect. Multimedia should come after the student understands the audience and purpose. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. Change every sentence into a shorter sentence#Incorrect. Shorter sentences can help, but the first step is identifying the publication audience and purpose.
=C. Identify the audience and what readers need to understand#Correct. Publication-ready writing starts with audience and purpose because those choices guide revision and multimedia. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~D. Export the file before revising it#Incorrect. Exporting comes after the writing has been revised and checked. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_L06_GP02::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A draft says, "This proves my point because it is important."
Which revision is strongest for publication?{
~A. This is very important and proves the whole point well.#Incorrect. This still uses vague language and does not explain the evidence. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=B. The character's decision supports the claim because it shows how conflict can reveal personal values.#Correct. This revision names the evidence and explains how it supports the claim. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~C. The point is proven because the reader can tell it is true.#Incorrect. This does not provide clear evidence or explanation. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. It is important because my topic is about important things.#Incorrect. This repeats vague wording instead of clarifying the analysis. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_L06_GP03::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student wants to publish a literary analysis paragraph online.
Which multimedia choice best supports the writing?{
=A. A labeled timeline showing the events discussed in the analysis#Correct. The timeline helps readers follow the evidence and understand the sequence connected to the analysis. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~B. A random image that makes the page look less empty#Incorrect. Publication multimedia should support understanding, not just fill space. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. An unrelated video because videos attract attention#Incorrect. Attention is not enough; the media must support the published writing. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. A decorative border around the paragraph#Incorrect. Decoration does not help readers understand the analysis. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_L06_GP04::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: Before exporting a piece for publication, a student reviews the final draft.
Which checklist item is most important?{
~A. The page has as many colors as possible#Incorrect. Many colors do not guarantee quality and may distract the reader. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. The file name is funny and memorable#Incorrect. A file name should be clear, but humor is not the main publication quality check.
~C. The writing uses complicated words in every sentence#Incorrect. Quality writing uses precise language, not unnecessary complexity. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=D. The writing is clear, revised, conventionally correct, and matched to the audience#Correct. This checklist addresses the main features of publication-ready writing. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_L06_GP05::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student says, "My audience is everyone."
What is the best feedback?{
~A. That is specific enough because everyone can read online writing.#Incorrect. A broad audience does not help the writer make clear revision choices. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. The audience should be changed to no one so the writing is easier.#Incorrect. Published writing must be created for an intended audience. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=C. Name a more specific audience so the writing, tone, and multimedia can be tailored.#Correct. A specific audience helps the writer revise content and choose multimedia purposefully. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~D. Audience does not matter once the writing is exported.#Incorrect. Audience matters before publication because it guides clarity, tone, and support.
}
